---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '@dunn.dev/reference/chrome/Hero.astro';
import SectionLabel from '@dunn.dev/reference/chrome/SectionLabel.astro';
---
<Layout description="A personal photo archive for Famly-using households. pascha pulls your kid's photos and videos from the nursery feed into your own Immich library with the post body, the educator's name, and the date embedded into each file via EXIF and XMP — so your child's history doesn't depend on Famly continuing to exist as a company.">
  <Hero title="pascha" eyebrow="photo archive · cli">
    <p>
      Photos and videos from your kid's daycare, kept in
      <em>your</em> Immich, in a form that outlives the vendor.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <a class="cta-primary" href="https://github.com/saidtezel/pascha">Read on GitHub →</a>
      <a class="cta-secondary" href="https://github.com/saidtezel/pascha/releases">Releases</a>
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionLabel label="What this is" />
    <p class="section-prose">
      Your daycare uses Famly. Every week the educators post a stream
      of photos and short videos of your kid: lunch, the sand pit, a
      collage they made on Wednesday, the moment they tried a new
      word. Famly is built for the in-the-moment surfacing of that
      stream. It is not built to be a long-term archive of your
      child's first years.
    </p>
    <p class="section-prose">
      <code>pascha</code> is a single Go binary that you run on your
      own machine, against your own household, with your own login.
      It pulls every photo and video the educators have posted,
      uploads them to your own Immich server, and embeds the caption, the educator's name, the kid tags, and the
      date into each file's own EXIF and XMP metadata. Any photo
      viewer or future archival workflow can read those fields —
      today, and in twenty years.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionLabel label="The vendor survives" />
    <p class="section-prose">
      The reason this matters is that an in-app feed is a handle to
      a service, not an artifact. When the company changes its mind
      about retention, sells to a different parent, deprecates the
      mobile app, or simply goes away, what you remember of your
      child's first three years of childcare goes with it.
    </p>
    <figure class="diagram">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 280" role="img" aria-label="Three stages in a horizontal flow: Famly's nursery feed on the left, the pascha binary in the middle, and your Immich library on the right. Three disruption events (outages, account closures, API changes) originate at Famly and terminate at a barrier between pascha and the disk, never reaching the archive.">
  <rect fill="none" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1" x="60" y="80" width="140" height="80" rx="4"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="14" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="0.06em" fill="var(--text-muted)" x="132" y="108" text-anchor="middle">FAMLY</text>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-2)" x="132" y="128" text-anchor="middle">nursery feed</text>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-2)" x="132" y="144" text-anchor="middle">photos, captions</text>
  <line fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5" x1="200" y1="120" x2="288" y2="120"/>
  <polygon fill="var(--accent)" points="288,120 280,116 280,124"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="0.04em" fill="var(--text-muted)" x="244" y="112" text-anchor="middle">PULL</text>
  <rect fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" x="288" y="80" width="140" height="80" rx="4"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="14" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="0.06em" fill="var(--accent)" x="360" y="108" text-anchor="middle">PASCHA</text>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-2)" x="360" y="128" text-anchor="middle">one binary</text>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-2)" x="360" y="144" text-anchor="middle">your machine</text>
  <line fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5" x1="428" y1="120" x2="516" y2="120"/>
  <polygon fill="var(--accent)" points="516,120 508,116 508,124"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="0.04em" fill="var(--text-muted)" x="472" y="112" text-anchor="middle">UPLOAD</text>
  <rect fill="none" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1" x="516" y="80" width="140" height="80" rx="4"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="14" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="0.06em" fill="var(--text-muted)" x="588" y="108" text-anchor="middle">YOUR IMMICH</text>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-2)" x="588" y="128" text-anchor="middle">self-hosted library</text>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-2)" x="588" y="144" text-anchor="middle">EXIF · XMP embedded</text>
  <line fill="none" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="2" x1="472" y1="174" x2="472" y2="268"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="10" font-weight="600" letter-spacing="0.08em" fill="var(--danger)" x="484" y="252" text-anchor="start">VENDOR</text>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="10" font-weight="600" letter-spacing="0.08em" fill="var(--danger)" x="484" y="266" text-anchor="start">SURVIVES</text>
  <path fill="none" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.25" stroke-dasharray="4 3" d="M132,160 L330,190 H464"/>
  <polygon fill="var(--danger)" points="472,190 464,186 464,194"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="0.04em" fill="var(--text-muted)" x="338" y="184" text-anchor="start">OUTAGES</text>
  <path fill="none" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.25" stroke-dasharray="4 3" d="M132,160 L290,214 H464"/>
  <polygon fill="var(--danger)" points="472,214 464,210 464,218"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="0.04em" fill="var(--text-muted)" x="298" y="208" text-anchor="start">ACCOUNT CLOSURES</text>
  <path fill="none" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.25" stroke-dasharray="4 3" d="M132,160 L250,238 H464"/>
  <polygon fill="var(--danger)" points="472,238 464,234 464,242"/>
  <text font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', Menlo, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="0.04em" fill="var(--text-muted)" x="258" y="232" text-anchor="start">API CHANGES</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="diagram-caption">disruption stops at the Immich boundary</p>
    </figure>
    <p class="section-prose">
      Once the bytes are in your Immich with metadata embedded, none
      of those vendor outcomes touches the history. The file is the
      artifact, not a handle to a service.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionLabel label="How pascha is shaped" />
    <div class="tile-grid">
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">flow · in</p>
        <p class="tile-headline">feed pages</p>
        <p class="tile-prose">
          Famly's parent-side GraphQL surface, walked one page at a
          time at the rate a single human user would naturally
          generate. Schema introspected from the live endpoint, types
          codegen'd, response shapes pinned by a drift baseline.
        </p>
        <p class="tile-meta">graphql · typed · introspected</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">discipline · posture</p>
        <p class="tile-headline">personal</p>
        <p class="tile-prose">
          One household, one operator, one set of credentials. No
          aggregation across families, no telemetry, no shared
          identifiers, no third-party calls. The binary doesn't know
          there is more than one of you.
        </p>
        <p class="tile-meta">one household · rate-limited</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">flow · out</p>
        <p class="tile-headline">files</p>
        <p class="tile-prose">
          JPEG and MP4 uploaded straight to your Immich server, with
          the caption, the educator's name, the kid tags, and the
          date embedded as EXIF and XMP. Nothing is kept on the
          machine running pascha; Immich is the archive.
        </p>
        <p class="tile-meta">immich only · no local copy</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">discipline · lifetime</p>
        <p class="tile-headline">archival</p>
        <p class="tile-prose">
          The file is the artifact, not a handle to a service. Open
          formats, standard metadata, no proprietary container.
          Readable in any year by any tool that has ever read a JPEG.
        </p>
        <p class="tile-meta">exif/xmp · open format · any year</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionLabel label="Why Famly" />
    <p class="section-prose">
      Vendors fall along a spectrum when an outside party builds
      against their surface. The <em>hostile</em> ones forbid access
      in their terms and chase violations. The <em>silent</em> ones
      forbid access but ignore violations and tolerate the drift. A
      third group — the <em>engaged</em> ones — restricts usage in
      their terms, acknowledges the use case in support interactions,
      and points users toward self-service solutions. Famly is in
      that third group, and that's what makes <code>pascha</code>
      possible: the surface is inspectable, the schema is
      introspectable, and the vendor's response when asked about
      export was to decline gracefully and point at the surface they
      already publish.
    </p>
    <p class="section-prose">
      This isn't a license — it's reputational, and the reciprocal
      obligation is to stay careful: one household, one operator, the
      rate a human generates, no aggregation, no resale, no agent
      farm. See <a href="https://andrew.dunn.dev/writing/progressive-vendor-stance/">a
      progressive vendor stance for the agent-native future</a> for
      the longer argument.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionLabel label="Install" />
    <p class="section-prose">
      Two ways to run <code>pascha</code>: build it with your own Go
      toolchain, or pull the container image that GitHub Actions
      builds from every push to main and every release tag.
    </p>
    <div class="tile-grid">
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">option a</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Go install</h3>
        <pre>go install github.com/saidtezel/pascha/cmd/pascha@latest</pre>
        <p class="tile-prose">
          Build from source against your local Go toolchain. Best
          for contributors and anyone who already runs Go for other
          work. Trust comes from the module proxy.
        </p>
        <p class="tile-meta">go 1.26+ · module-cached</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">option b</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Container image</h3>
        <pre>docker pull ghcr.io/saidtezel/pascha:latest</pre>
        <p class="tile-prose">
          A static binary on a distroless base, published to the
          GitHub Container Registry. Best for cron on a homelab box
          where you don't want Go or a loose binary on disk. No volume
          needed: pascha keeps no state. Pass the Famly credentials and
          the Immich URL and API key as env vars, and set
          <code>PASCHA_INTERVAL=6h</code> to keep it running as a
          service that fetches on that interval.
        </p>
        <p class="tile-meta">linux/amd64 · linux/arm64 · rootless-ok</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionLabel label="Who this is for" />
    <p class="section-prose">
      A parent, co-parent, or household admin whose kid's daycare
      uses Famly, runs Immich, and wants a permanent copy of every
      photo and video the educators post, with metadata that survives any
      future tooling change.
    </p>
    <p class="section-prose">
      If your school doesn't use Famly, or if you're happy enough
      with how the mobile app surfaces history, <code>pascha</code>
      is not for you. It's also not multi-tenant — one binary, one
      household, one Immich.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <SectionLabel label="What this isn't" />
    <p class="section-prose">
      Not a privacy gateway. Every piece of context the vendor
      exposes lands inside the file you keep — that's the whole
      point of EXIF and XMP for archival. What happens to those
      files after you have them is your call. Not a scraping service
      either: one operator, the rate of one human, written to feel
      like a parent backing up their own photos because that's what
      it is.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
